  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14133661/assets/081dab1fcd5ccde5281ec4369417c4b0911a404695e9d147b046daf68f8621a6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-5.$ The function reaches this value at   $(x,y)=(0,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14133661/assets/c1fb4d47cc10c74537e01803a633fd9f8d6c9b1c6f8b8cd945bfba058861c2d1.png)",
